.TH JENT_ENTROPY_SWITCH_NOTIME_IMPL 3 2026-09-30 "jitterentropy x.y.z" "Library Functions Manual"
.\" groff re-enables hyphenation per paragraph unless HY is 0.
.nr HY 0
.nh
.SH NAME
jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl, jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu, jent_notime_init, jent_notime_fini \- timer-less mode of the CPU Jitter RNG
.SH LIBRARY
Jitter RNG library
.RI ( libjitterentropy ", " \-ljitterentropy ).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <jitterentropy.h>
.sp
.BI "struct jent_notime_thread {"
.BI "        int (*jent_notime_init)(void **" ctx );
.BI "        void (*jent_notime_fini)(void *" ctx );
.BI "        int (*jent_notime_start)(void *" ctx ", start_routine, void *" arg );
.BI "        void (*jent_notime_stop)(void *" ctx );
.BI "};"
.sp
.BI "int jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl(struct jent_notime_thread *" new_thread );
.sp
.BI "int jent_notime_init(void **" ctx );
.sp
.BI "void jent_notime_fini(void *" ctx );
.sp
.BI "int jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu(unsigned long " cpu ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
allows the caller to set a thread handler that is used by the
Jitter RNG if it operates in the timer-less mode.
.I jent_notime_init
sets up the handler's context in
.IR *ctx ,
and
.I jent_notime_fini
releases it; it is only invoked with the non-NULL context a successful
.I jent_notime_init
stored.
.I jent_notime_start
starts a thread running
.I start_routine
with
.IR arg ,
as the trailing two parameters of
.BR pthread_create (3)
do, and
.I jent_notime_stop
stops it; it is called exactly once for every start that succeeded. The
Jitter RNG starts and stops the thread frequently.
.IR start_routine 's
type is that of
.BR pthread_create (3)
where the library uses POSIX threads, and
.I "int (*)(void *)"
otherwise.
.BR jent_notime_init ()
and
.BR jent_notime_fini ()
are the library's own implementations of the first two, exported so that a
handler can reuse them rather than duplicate them. They start a thread only
where the library has a thread backend of its own - not in the kernel builds
and not in a freestanding one, whose handler has to bring its own.
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
must be called before
.BR jent_entropy_init (3)
as after this call, the change of the thread handler is denied.
.LP
.BR jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu ()
pins the counting thread of the timer-less mode to the logical CPU index
.IR cpu .
When it is not set, that thread runs on the highest-numbered CPU in the
affinity set of the thread that starts it - the caller's, which a cpuset or
job object may confine; outside Linux and Windows, where no affinity set can
be read, on the CPU count minus one. As with the thread handler above, it must be called before
.BR jent_entropy_init (3):
afterwards the call has no effect and returns
.IR \-EAGAIN .
The pinning is advisory - an index the platform cannot honor, and a platform
with no thread-to-CPU affinity API at all (OpenBSD, macOS on Apple Silicon),
leave the counting thread where the scheduler puts it rather than failing the
call. A CPU outside the process's affinity mask or cpuset is one such index.
On Windows the index counts the active processors of all processor groups in
group order, and a CPU in a processor group other than the counting thread's
is not honored either. It returns
.IR 0
on success and a negative errno otherwise.
.LP
Without the internal timer compiled in, both functions above return
.IR \-EOPNOTSUPP .
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
and
.BR jent_entropy_set_notime_cpu ()
return 0 on success,
.I \-EAGAIN
once an initialization has run, and
.I \-EOPNOTSUPP
without the internal timer.
.BR jent_entropy_switch_notime_impl ()
returns
.I \-EINVAL
for a NULL handler or one with a NULL callback.
.PP
.BR jent_notime_init ()
returns 0 on success,
.I \-ENOENT
on a system with fewer than two CPUs or without a thread backend of the
library's own,
.I \-ENOMEM
when its context cannot be allocated, and any negative errno the CPU count
query fails with. Without the internal timer it is a stub returning 0.
.BR jent_notime_fini ()
returns no value.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
See
.B THREAD SAFETY
in
.BR jitterentropy (3)
for the details.
.ad l
.TS
allbox;
lbx lb
lx l.
Interface	Thread safety
T{
\%\fBjent_entropy_switch_notime_impl\fP(), \%\fBjent_entropy_set_notime_cpu\fP()
T}	MT-Unsafe init
T{
\%\fBjent_notime_init\fP(), \%\fBjent_notime_fini\fP()
T}	MT-Safe race:ctx
.TE
.ad
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3),
.BR jent_entropy_init (3),
.BR jitterentropy (3),
